                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Research the effects of chlorine on the human body.\n2. Determine the recommended safe levels of chlorine in drinking water.\n3. Find out how long chlorine remains effective in water.\n4. Determine if the chlorine levels in week old water are still within safe limits.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. Chlorine is commonly used to disinfect water and kill harmful bacteria. However, consuming high levels of chlorine can cause negative health effects such as stomach discomfort, nausea, and vomiting.\n2. The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) sets the maximum contaminant level (MCL) for chlorine in drinking water at 4 milligrams per liter (mg/L).\n3. According to the EPA, chlorine remains effective in water for up to 24 hours.\n4. If the chlorine levels in week old water have not been replenished, it is likely that the levels have decreased below the safe limit of 4 mg/L. Therefore, it is not safe to drink week old chlorine water.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
