                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Oysters are a type of mollusk that are commonly consumed as food. Guitar manufacturing involves the use of various materials, including wood, metal, and plastic.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What materials are typically used in guitar manufacturing?\n2. Can oyster shells be used to make any of these materials?\n3. Are there any examples of oyster shells being used in guitar manufacturing?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \n1. The materials typically used in guitar manufacturing include wood for the body and neck, metal for the hardware (such as the tuning pegs and bridge), and plastic for the pickguard and other components.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\n2. Oyster shells can be used to make a material called mother-of-pearl, which is often used for inlay work on guitars. Mother-of-pearl is made from the inner layer of oyster shells, which is composed of calcium carbonate and has a pearly luster.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3:\n3. Yes, there are examples of oyster shells being used in guitar manufacturing. For example, some guitar makers use mother-of-pearl inlays on the fretboard or headstock of their instruments. Additionally, some guitar picks are made from a material called \"oyster shell celluloid,\" which is a type of plastic that contains ground-up oyster shells for added strength and durability.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nBased on the information above, the answer to the original question is: True, oysters can be used in guitar manufacturing, specifically in the form of mother-of-pearl for inlay work and oyster shell celluloid for guitar picks.",
