                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain the main difference between planets and moons in our solar system?\n\nProfessor: Sure. The main difference is that all planets orbit the Sun, while all moons orbit planets.\n\nStudent: So, does that mean moons cannot exist without planets?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Moons are natural satellites that orbit planets, and they are held in place by the planet's gravity.\n\nStudent: What about the size difference between planets and moons?\n\nProfessor: Well, there is no set size difference between planets and moons. Some moons are larger than some planets, but in general, planets are larger than moons.\n\nStudent: Does the ability to support life have anything to do with the difference between planets and moons?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it does. While some moons may have conditions that could potentially support life, in general, planets are more likely to have the necessary conditions for life to exist.\n\nStudent: And what about atmospheres?\n\nProfessor: Planets are more likely to have atmospheres than moons, although there are some exceptions. An atmosphere is a layer of gases that surrounds a planet or moon, and it is held in place by the planet's or moon's gravity.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining all of that. I think I understand the main difference between planets and moons now.",
