                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: What is the main factor that determines Earth's orbit within the solar system?\n\nHelpful knowledge: The solar system consists of the Sun, eight planets, and various other celestial bodies. The planets orbit the Sun in elliptical paths. The force of gravity is what keeps the planets in their orbits.\n\nAdditional facts: Earth is the third planet from the Sun and has a nearly circular orbit. The Moon is Earth's only natural satellite and has a significant impact on Earth's tides.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the force that keeps planets in their orbits?\nAnswer: The force of gravity.\n\n2. What is the position of Earth in relation to the Sun?\nAnswer: Earth is the third planet from the Sun.\n\n3. What is the shape of Earth's orbit?\nAnswer: Earth's orbit is nearly circular.\n\n4. What is the impact of the Moon on Earth's orbit?\nAnswer: The Moon has a significant impact on Earth's tides.\n\nAnswer: The main factor that determines Earth's orbit within the solar system is the mass of the Sun. The force of gravity between the Sun and Earth keeps Earth in its nearly circular orbit. While the Moon does have an impact on Earth's tides, it is not the main factor that determines Earth's orbit. The pulling force of other planets and the distance between them can have some impact on Earth's orbit, but the mass of the Sun is the primary factor.",
