                        "cot": "\n\nGravity is a force of attraction between objects, so it is most likely to influence systems that involve objects with mass. \n\nA) Surface water evaporating into the atmosphere is not an example of gravity influencing other systems. This process is driven by heat energy from the Sun, which causes water molecules to gain enough energy to break free from the surface and rise into the atmosphere. Gravity does play a role in the water cycle, as it causes precipitation to fall back to the surface, but it is not the primary force at work in this process.\n\nB) Planets moving around the Sun in an elliptical pattern is a classic example of gravity influencing other systems. The gravitational pull of the Sun keeps the planets in orbit around it, and the shape of their orbits is determined by the balance between the force of gravity and the planets' own momentum.\n\nC) Glaciers melting because of increasing temperatures is not directly related to gravity. The melting of glaciers is primarily driven by changes in temperature and climate, which can cause ice to melt and flow downhill. While gravity does play a role in the movement of glaciers, it is not the primary force at work in causing them to melt.\n\nD) Groundwater moving to the surface in an artesian well is also an example of gravity influencing other systems. The movement of groundwater is driven by the force of gravity, which causes water to flow downhill from areas of higher elevation to areas of lower elevation. In an artesian well, the pressure of the water is enough to push it to the surface against the force of gravity, but this pressure is ultimately caused by the weight of the water in the surrounding aquifer. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) planets moving around the Sun in an elliptical pattern.",
