                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what you mean by \"systems\" in this context?\n\nProfessor: Sure, by \"systems\" I mean any collection of objects that interact with each other. For example, the Earth and the Moon can be considered a system because they interact with each other through gravity.\n\nStudent: Okay, got it. So, which of the options is an example of gravity influencing other systems?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is B) planets moving around the Sun in an elliptical pattern. This is because the gravitational force between the Sun and the planets is what keeps the planets in their orbits.\n\nStudent: How does gravity keep the planets in their orbits?\n\nProfessor: Well, gravity is a force of attraction between objects that have mass. The more massive an object is, the stronger its gravitational pull. In the case of the planets, the Sun's massive gravitational pull keeps them in their orbits.\n\nStudent: What do you mean by \"elliptical pattern\"?\n\nProfessor: An elliptical pattern is a type of oval shape. In the case of the planets, their orbits around the Sun are not perfectly circular, but rather they follow an elliptical path.\n\nStudent: Why do the planets follow an elliptical path instead of a circular one?\n\nProfessor: That's a great question. The reason for this is because the gravitational force between the Sun and the planets is not constant. It varies depending on the distance between them. As the planets move closer to the Sun, the gravitational force increases, causing them to speed up. As they move farther away, the gravitational force decreases, causing them to slow down. This variation in gravitational force causes the planets to follow an elliptical path around the Sun.",
