                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The atmospheric layer in which the summit of Mount Everest is located can be determined by understanding how temperature changes with altitude. Since the temperature decreases as altitude increases, we know that the summit of Mount Everest must be located in a layer where temperatures are very low. \n\nThe atmospheric layers are defined by their temperature profiles, so we need to identify which layer has the lowest temperatures at high altitudes. The layers, in order from lowest to highest altitude, are the troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, and thermosphere. \n\nThe troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere, extending from the Earth's surface up to an altitude of about 7-20 km. This is where weather occurs and where temperature generally decreases with altitude. The stratosphere is the layer above the troposphere, extending from about 20-50 km. This layer contains the ozone layer and has a temperature that increases with altitude due to the absorption of UV radiation by ozone. The mesosphere is the layer above the stratosphere, extending from about 50-85 km. This layer has a very low temperature, with the coldest temperatures in the entire atmosphere occurring near the top of the mesosphere. Finally, the thermosphere is the highest layer of the atmosphere, extending from about 85 km up to the edge of space. This layer has a very high temperature due to the absorption of solar radiation. \n\nSince we know that the temperature decreases with altitude on Mount Everest, we can eliminate the stratosphere and thermosphere as possible answers. The troposphere is too low in altitude to contain the summit of Mount Everest. Therefore, the only remaining option is the mesosphere. Therefore, the summit of Mount Everest is located in the mesosphere. \n\nThe answer is A) mesosphere.",
