                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me how we can determine which atmospheric layer the summit of Mount Everest is in based on the temperature?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the temperature decreases as the altitude increases because the air pressure decreases. This means that the air molecules are more spread out and cannot hold heat as well. \n\nStudent: Okay, so how does this help us determine which atmospheric layer the summit is in?\n\nProfessor: Well, each atmospheric layer has a different temperature profile. The layer closest to the Earth's surface is the troposphere, where we live and where weather occurs. The temperature decreases with altitude in this layer. \n\nStudent: Got it. So, is the summit of Mount Everest in the troposphere?\n\nProfessor: No, actually. The next layer up is the stratosphere, where the temperature increases with altitude due to the presence of the ozone layer. So, the summit of Mount Everest is not in the stratosphere either. \n\nStudent: Okay, so which layer is it in then?\n\nProfessor: The summit of Mount Everest is actually in the troposphere, despite the fact that the temperature decreases with altitude in this layer. The troposphere extends up to about 7-20 km above the Earth's surface, and the summit of Mount Everest is at 8,848 meters, which is within the troposphere. \n\nStudent: I see. Thank you for explaining that to me, Professor.",
