                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Cold air is denser than warm air and therefore tends to sink. Mountains can create areas of high pressure due to the air being forced upward and compressing. Valleys can create areas of low pressure due to the air being able to spread out and expand.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What happens to air when it is forced upward?\n2. How does air pressure change with altitude?\n3. What is the relationship between temperature and air density?\n4. How does air flow from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure?\n\nAnswers:\n1. When air is forced upward, it expands and cools, which can cause moisture to condense and form clouds.\n2. Air pressure decreases with altitude, meaning that the air is less dense at higher elevations.\n3. Cold air is denser than warm air, so cold air will tend to sink and flow toward areas of lower pressure.\n4. Air flows from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure, which is why cold air at the tops of mountains will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys. Therefore, the answer is A) will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys.",
