                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "How can we explain the decrease in force of a hurricane as it moves over land?\n\nTo answer this question, we need to understand the factors that contribute to the strength of a hurricane. Hurricanes are fueled by warm, moist air, which rises and condenses to form clouds and precipitation. As the warm air rises, it creates an area of low pressure at the surface, which draws in more warm, moist air from the surrounding area. This process continues as long as there is a supply of warm, moist air to fuel the storm.\n\nWhen a hurricane moves over land, it loses its source of warm, moist air. This is because land does not hold as much moisture as the ocean, and the friction between the storm and the land surface slows down the winds. As a result, the supply of warm, moist air decreases, and the storm begins to weaken.\n\nTherefore, the statement that best explains why the force of a hurricane diminishes as the hurricane moves over land is D) The supply of warm, moist air decreases.",
