                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Lightbulbs work by passing an electric current through a filament, which heats up and produces light.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. How does a lightbulb produce light?\n2. What happens to the electrical energy when it passes through the filament?\n3. What other forms of energy could be produced when the electrical energy passes through the filament?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A lightbulb produces light by passing an electric current through a filament, which heats up and produces light.\n2. When the electrical energy passes through the filament, it is converted into heat energy. This causes the filament to heat up and produce light.\n3. The only other form of energy that could be produced when the electrical energy passes through the filament is heat energy. No chemical, magnetic, or mechanical energy is produced.",
