                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain how electrical energy is changed into light energy when a lightbulb is lit?\n\nProfessor: Sure, when an electric current flows through the filament of a lightbulb, it heats up and becomes so hot that it emits light. This process is called incandescence.\n\nStudent: So, does that mean the answer is C) heat energy?\n\nProfessor: No, the answer is actually B) magnetic energy. When the electric current flows through the filament, it creates a magnetic field that causes the filament to heat up and emit light.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. How does the magnetic field cause the filament to heat up?\n\nProfessor: The magnetic field causes the electrons in the filament to move back and forth rapidly, which generates heat. This heat causes the filament to glow and emit light.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Thank you for explaining it step by step, Professor.\n\nProfessor: You're welcome, always happy to help. Do you have any other questions?",
