                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Michael Faraday was a British scientist who made significant contributions to the fields of electromagnetism and electrochemistry in the 19th century.\n\nAdditional fact: Faraday's work on electromagnetic induction led to the development of the first electric generator.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is electric current?\n2. How does electric current flow through a wire?\n3. What is a magnetic field?\n4. How can electric current produce a magnetic field?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Electric current is the flow of electric charge through a conductor.\n2. Electric current flows through a wire when there is a potential difference (voltage) between two points on the wire, causing electrons to move from the negative terminal to the positive terminal.\n3. A magnetic field is a region in space where a magnetic force can be detected.\n4. Electric current produces a magnetic field because moving charges (electrons) create a magnetic field around them. When a wire carries an electric current, the magnetic field produced by the moving electrons creates a circular magnetic field around the wire. This is known as the right-hand rule, where the direction of the magnetic field is perpendicular to the direction of the current flow and the direction of the curl of the fingers of the right hand.",
                        "answers": [
