                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain how electric current in a wire produces a magnetic field?\n\nProfessor: Sure, let me break it down for you. When an electric current flows through a wire, it creates a magnetic field around the wire.\n\nStudent: How does the electric current create a magnetic field?\n\nProfessor: The electric current is made up of moving electrons, and when these electrons move through the wire, they create a magnetic field around the wire.\n\nStudent: What is a magnetic field?\n\nProfessor: A magnetic field is a force field that surrounds a magnet or a wire carrying an electric current. It is the area where the magnetic force is exerted.\n\nStudent: How does the magnetic field affect other objects?\n\nProfessor: The magnetic field can attract or repel other magnets or magnetic materials. It can also induce an electric current in a nearby wire or conductor.\n\nStudent: How did Michael Faraday discover this phenomenon?\n\nProfessor: Michael Faraday discovered this phenomenon through a series of experiments where he observed the interaction between electric currents and magnets. He found that when a wire carrying an electric current was placed near a magnet, it would experience a force and move. This led him to conclude that electric currents create magnetic fields.",
